  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Sun names from the north",
      "content": "<p>Old English and Old Norse both used <em>sól</em> or <em>sunne</em> for the sun, and a few names built on them are still around. Sunniva ('sun gift') is the Norwegian form of an Old English name. Solveig, 'sun strength', is well known in Scandinavia, and Solfrid is a 19th-century Norwegian coinage. They're unusual in England but easy to say and spell.</p>"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Pairing and nicknames",
      "content": "<p>Short sun names (Sol, Ravi, Shams, Inti) suit longer surnames, and three-syllable ones like Sunniva, Savitri and Heliodoro suit short ones. Sunniva and Solveig shorten naturally to Sunny and Sol, which gives a child a choice between a formal name and an everyday one.</p>"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Day names",
      "content": "<p>Sunday is a 'day name', given to a child born on that day. The habit is strongest in parts of West Africa, where Sunday and Monday are used for boys and girls. If you like the idea, it carries the sun meaning through Old English <em>sunnandæg</em>, 'sun's day'.</p>"
    }
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "What girl names mean sun?",
      "answer": "Soleil (French), Sunniva ('sun gift'), Solveig ('sun strength'), Heulwen (Welsh, 'sunshine'), Saulė (Lithuanian), Arev (Armenian) and Güneş (Turkish). Sol, Spanish for sun, is mainly a girl's name in Spanish."
    },
    {
      "question": "What boy names mean sun?",
      "answer": "Samson comes from the Hebrew word for sun. Ravi, Surya and Suraj mean sun in Sanskrit and Hindi, Helios in Greek, and Tonatiuh in Nahuatl. Shams (Arabic) and Inti (Quechua) are also used."
    },
    {
      "question": "What name means sunshine?",
      "answer": "Heulwen in Welsh and Shandiin in Navajo both mean sunshine. Sunshine itself is also used as an English word name."
    }
  ],
